content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-perth.md-101-1. **Initial consultation and assessment** — The reader meets their surgeon, talks through medical history and goals, and gets an honest view of whether the sleeve suits them. This is also where they can ask the questions that feel awkward to raise.
content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-perth.md-102-2. **Pre-surgery preparation** — The patient may need to follow a specific pre-op diet (typically 2–4 weeks) to shrink the liver and reduce surgical risk. Emphasise that the team supports them through this — not just a handout.
content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-perth.md-103-3. **The procedure** — General anaesthetic, keyhole technique, 45–60 minutes. Surgeon removes ~75–80% of the stomach and seals the new sleeve with a staple line. Keep it clinical but accessible.
content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:104:4. **Hospital recovery** — Around 2 nights at St John of God Murdoch or Subiaco. Patient starts on fluids and progresses under guidance from nursing and dietetic staff.
content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-perth.md-105-5. **Returning to daily life** — Desk work after ~2 weeks; physical or manual labour closer to 4–6 weeks. Surgeon provides advice tailored to the individual's situation.
content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-perth.md-106-6. **Long-term follow-up** — Staged eating progression (fluids → soft → regular textures) with dietitian support. Ongoing reviews and blood tests to protect nutrition and results long-term.
content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-perth.md-107-
--
content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-perth.md-111-Each bullet should convey one clear, qualified benefit. Do not use superlatives.
content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-perth.md-112-
content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-perth.md-113-1. Weight loss — clinical averages of 60–70% of excess weight over 12–18 months are reported in some studies; individual results vary and no result is guaranteed.
content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:114:2. Reduced appetite — the procedure lowers ghrelin production, and some people may find smaller portions more satisfying; individual experiences vary.
content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-perth.md-115-3. Improvement in weight-related conditions — type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, joint pain. Qualify that outcomes differ between individuals.
content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:116:4. No intestinal rerouting — food travels through the digestive tract in its normal order; the sleeve is generally less malabsorptive than a bypass, although vitamin and mineral monitoring is still needed.
content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:117:5. Keyhole approach — smaller incisions than open surgery; pain, hospital stay and recovery vary by individual and surgical circumstances.
content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-perth.md-118-6. Single permanent procedure — no implanted device to adjust or maintain, unlike a gastric band.
