    96	---
    97	
    98	### How it works (6 steps)
    99	Each step gets a short heading and 1–2 sentences of content direction.
   100	
   101	1. **Initial consultation and assessment** — The reader meets their surgeon, talks through medical history and goals, and gets an honest view of whether the sleeve suits them. This is also where they can ask the questions that feel awkward to raise.
   102	2. **Pre-surgery preparation** — The patient may need to follow a specific pre-op diet (typically 2–4 weeks) to shrink the liver and reduce surgical risk. Emphasise that the team supports them through this — not just a handout.
   103	3. **The procedure** — General anaesthetic, keyhole technique, 45–60 minutes. Surgeon removes ~75–80% of the stomach and seals the new sleeve with a staple line. Keep it clinical but accessible.
   104	4. **Hospital recovery** — Around 2 nights at St John of God Murdoch or Subiaco. Patient starts on fluids and progresses under guidance from nursing and dietetic staff.
   105	5. **Returning to daily life** — Desk work after ~2 weeks; physical or manual labour closer to 4–6 weeks. Surgeon provides advice tailored to the individual's situation.
   106	6. **Long-term follow-up** — Staged eating progression (fluids → soft → regular textures) with dietitian support. Ongoing reviews and blood tests to protect nutrition and results long-term.
   107	
   108	---
   109	
   110	### Benefits (6 bullets)
   111	Each bullet should convey one clear, qualified benefit. Do not use superlatives.
   112	
   113	1. Weight loss — clinical averages of 60–70% of excess weight over 12–18 months are reported in some studies; individual results vary and no result is guaranteed.
   114	2. Reduced appetite — the procedure lowers ghrelin production, and some people may find smaller portions more satisfying; individual experiences vary.
   115	3. Improvement in weight-related conditions — type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, joint pain. Qualify that outcomes differ between individuals.
   116	4. No intestinal rerouting — food travels through the digestive tract in its normal order; the sleeve is generally less malabsorptive than a bypass, although vitamin and mineral monitoring is still needed.
   117	5. Keyhole approach — smaller incisions than open surgery; pain, hospital stay and recovery vary by individual and surgical circumstances.
   118	6. Single permanent procedure — no implanted device to adjust or maintain, unlike a gastric band.
   119	
   120	---
   121	
   122	### Risks / honest section
   123	- **Intro paragraph tone:** Acknowledge directly that gastric sleeve surgery is major surgery with real risks. The practice would rather the reader hear these plainly now than be surprised later. The surgeon will go through each risk in the context of the individual's health before any decision is made.
   124	- **Risk bullets to address:**
   125	  1. Staple line leak — occurs in roughly 1–3% of cases; the most serious early complication, monitored closely and may require further treatment.
   126	  2. Reflux or heartburn — can be new or worsened after a sleeve; symptoms may settle for some and persist for others; it may influence the choice of procedure.
   127	  3. Nutritional deficiencies — particularly vitamin B12, iron and vitamin D; lifelong supplements and regular blood tests are part of staying well.
   128	  4. Hair thinning — can occur in the months after surgery and may be associated with rapid weight loss or reduced nutritional intake; patients should discuss persistent symptoms or nutritional concerns with their care team.
   129	  5. Weight regain — possible if eating habits drift and follow-up lapses; the sleeve works best alongside ongoing support.
   130	  6. General surgical risks — bleeding, infection, blood clots and anaesthetic reactions, as with any operation.
   131	
   132	---
   133	
   134	### Cost paragraph
   135	Cover these points in a single flowing paragraph (not a list):
   136	- Total cost varies depending on private health cover, surgeon and anaesthetist fees, hospital charges and any out-of-pocket gap.
   137	- Signal honesty about cost upfront — uncertainty about money is a major source of stress.
   138	- Medicare item number 31575 attracts a rebate when eligibility criteria are met.
   139	- Private health insurance with appropriate hospital cover may contribute to hospital and theatre costs; benefits, waiting periods and gaps depend on the policy and fund.
   140	- A cost guide breaks down components and funding options including private health insurance and early-access superannuation for eligible cases.
   141	- Do not quote a single dollar figure.
   142	
